    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, I had the true honor of serving John McCain, traveling with him, primarily to Europe, where I saw that he was not only a national hero in America, but he was the national hero in so many Eastern European countries where he fought for individual liberty, for greater democracy, individual freedom. And I also saw the scars You know, the disabilities that John suffered for a lifetime because of his service as a nation. We would have to help John out with his coat. We'd have to put his suitcase up into the overhead. You know, somebody had to comb his hair. So, you know, when you actually served with a man, you recognized the enormous sacrifice. And, and, truthfully, you heard the story firsthand. I mean, how many people literally, when you're in a cage in Vietnam, knowing what, don't know whether you ever get out, knowing that you're going to seem to be tortured, and when you refuse to go home when you're given the opportunity your punishment be probably increased how many would people would choose to stay and do that high integrity on a ball thing so i mean to me john mccain was a hero i i didn't agree with them in many cases but there was no denying that he was a true american hero he's irreplaceable he was a figure larger than life i i i i didn't like i did not like seeing anybody politicize the that time of morning of celebrating a life very well lived. So again, it was sad, it was a somber time, but I tried to look at those aspects of John McCain that were uplifting. Somebody who served the cause greater than self for decades, that's something to celebrate, that's something to appreciate, and certainly nothing to politicize.",
